WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.330
Last month, a student lost

2
00:00:02.330 --> 00:00:03.250
his game account.

3
00:00:03.950 --> 00:00:05.890
The thief had his password, but

4
00:00:05.890 --> 00:00:07.790
the game also asked for a code

5
00:00:07.790 --> 00:00:09.090
sent to the student’s phone.

6
00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:12.820
So the thief wrote: “Oops, I

7
00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:14.540
sent my code to your number.

8
00:00:15.190 --> 00:00:16.450
Can you forward it?”

9
00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:19.000
He did, and the account was gone.

10
00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:23.140
Today: two-step sign-in, and why a

11
00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:25.580
code is a key you never hand over.

12
00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:28.520
With two-step sign-in, your

13
00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:30.560
password isn’t enough on its own.

14
00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:33.820
A new phone signing in also needs a

15
00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:36.840
second step: a code, a tap on a

16
00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:38.060
prompt, or your fingerprint.

17
00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:40.180
A thief with only your

18
00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:41.560
password gets stuck.

19
00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:44.660
There are three kinds of proof.

20
00:00:45.380 --> 00:00:47.500
Something you know, like a password.

21
00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:50.480
Something you have, like your phone.

22
00:00:51.130 --> 00:00:52.950
And something you are, like

23
00:00:52.950 --> 00:00:54.590
your fingerprint or face.

24
00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:56.860
Two different kinds together

25
00:00:56.860 --> 00:00:58.500
are much harder to steal.

26
00:00:59.710 --> 00:01:00.950
So here’s the rule.

27
00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:04.460
A one-time code is a key for you alone.

28
00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:08.020
Nobody real ever asks you to read it

29
00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:11.120
out or forward it: not a friend, not

30
00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:12.740
the game, not the bank.

31
00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:16.300
Turn it on, with a parent if you

32
00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:19.000
like, starting with your email: it

33
00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:20.300
can reset everything else.

34
00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:23.720
In WhatsApp, set a six-digit PIN

35
00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:25.280
under Two-step verification.

36
00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:28.500
Games and social apps keep it in

37
00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:29.600
their security settings.

38
00:01:30.980 --> 00:01:31.560
Quick check!

39
00:01:32.350 --> 00:01:35.610
A game friend writes: “I sent my

40
00:01:35.610 --> 00:01:37.550
code to your phone by mistake.

41
00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:39.900
Please send it to me.”

42
00:01:40.550 --> 00:01:41.810
What do you do?

43
00:01:43.270 --> 00:01:44.190
Don’t send it.

44
00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:46.740
The code came to your phone because

45
00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:48.780
someone is trying to sign in to your

46
00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:49.080
account.

47
00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:52.240
Whoever asks for it wants your account.

48
00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:55.780
Some apps ask instead: “Is

49
00:01:55.780 --> 00:01:57.260
this you signing in?”

50
00:01:57.820 --> 00:01:59.880
Only tap yes if it really

51
00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:00.960
is you, right now.

52
00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:04.210
Passkeys go further: you sign in

53
00:02:04.210 --> 00:02:06.550
with your fingerprint or face, so

54
00:02:06.550 --> 00:02:07.910
there’s no password to steal.

55
00:02:09.220 --> 00:02:10.520
What if you lose your phone?

56
00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:13.380
When you turn on two-step sign-in,

57
00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:15.200
you’re often given backup codes.

58
00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:17.830
Write them on paper and keep them

59
00:02:17.830 --> 00:02:19.610
safe at home, not in your phone.

60
00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:21.600
Your turn.

61
00:02:22.220 --> 00:02:24.800
At night, your phone asks: “Is

62
00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:25.860
this you signing in?”

63
00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:28.380
But you’re in bed, not

64
00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:29.240
signing in anywhere.

65
00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:30.700
What do you do?

66
00:02:31.910 --> 00:02:33.910
Tap no: someone has your

67
00:02:33.910 --> 00:02:35.550
password, so change it now.

68
00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:38.020
Remember: turn on two-step

69
00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:40.580
sign-in, never share a code, and

70
00:02:40.580 --> 00:02:42.620
keep backup codes safe at home.

71
00:02:43.810 --> 00:02:46.010
Try it tomorrow: with a parent or

72
00:02:46.010 --> 00:02:48.510
guardian, turn on two-step sign-in

73
00:02:48.510 --> 00:02:50.050
for your email or WhatsApp.

74
00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:53.030
Write the backup codes or the PIN on

75
00:02:53.030 --> 00:02:55.650
paper, and keep it somewhere safe at home.
